A for-loop in a visual tree becomes a slot the html platform re-renders. The
emission is an accumulator `__slot0` holding the nodes the loop placed, a
`__renderSlot0(parent)` that walks `state.items` against what is already there,
and a `data-sngl-slot` anchor marking where they go. The initial render calls it
at bootstrap; a handler that mutates `items` calls it again, and that second
call is what makes the loop reactive.

The row is a component the compiler wrote, which is what makes the render a
placement rather than a rebuild. `__place0_i_ref0 === __n1__el` is the cursor
test, and it can only match a node the render hands back the same object for --
so while the row was a `document.createElement` two lines above the comparison,
that branch was dead and every row was re-inserted on every render. The
instance registry is what gives the row an identity across renders; `__set___p0`
is what lets it be told the new value instead of being built again.

Each is emitted once, which it was not until this golden showed it. A page
whose root is a component and not an explicit `window` is served as a window
whose Funcs are that component's, so every synthesized func reached
synthesizedFuncs from two sources -- and both loops that consume that list
ran, defining __renderSlot0 twice and calling it twice at startup, the second
call removing the nodes the first had just built.

The handler is the other thing that changed. `items = items.push("new")`
compiled to `state.items = state.items.push("new")`, and JavaScript's push
answers the new length -- so the list became 4 on the first click and the
re-render walked a number. It is the bare call now; list_push_is_a_statement
covers the rule across all three languages.
-- app.sngl --
import . "sngl:ui"

output { none { html } }

component main node {
    var items list<string> = ["a", "b", "c"]
    button(text="add", @click { items.push("new") })
    for var item = items {
        text(value=item)
    }
}

window {
    main
}
